    "math_academy_id": "q-72424",
    "knowledge_point_id": "e2b47545-fdf1-5e6a-b0bb-bbbecb8067cc",
    "knowledge_point": "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Positive Integer Exponent",
    "problem": "$\\int 16(7-2x)^{7}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
    "worked_solution": "Let $u=7-2x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 16(7-2x)^{7}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 16{u}^{7}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =16\\int {u}^{7}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\int {u}^{7}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{8}}{8}+C \\\\  & =-{u}^{8}+C \\\\  & =-(7-2x)^{8}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-(7-2x)^{8}+C] & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(7-2x)^{8}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-8(7-2x)^{7}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7-2x)+0 \\\\  & =-8(7-2x)^{7}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =16(7-2x)^{7}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$",
